{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Downward bias needed: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices across the whole 30d horizon (typical positive error on the order of ~+2 to +6 €/t, larger in mid-horizon). Apply a level shift down to the full path.",
    "Trend/lag error: in selloffs the model reacts too slowly (forecasts stay elevated while spot continues falling). Add a momentum/trend-following adjustment: if the last 10–15 days show a clear downtrend, tilt the forecast path downward more aggressively, especially days 5–20.",
    "Horizon shape: errors are not just day-1; they widen into the mid-horizon (≈days 10–20) where the model overestimates the most. Use a horizon-dependent correction: larger negative adjustment mid-horizon, smaller near day 1 and day 30.",
    "Mean-reversion overshoot: after local rebounds/spikes in history, the model projects too much bounce/mean reversion and keeps prices too high. Damp rebound amplitude (shrink deviations from recent mean) and cap upside retracement following a drawdown.",
    "Late-horizon underreaction to stabilization: when the realized path flattens/recovers late in the window, the model still tends to be too high but the gap narrows. Reduce correction magnitude toward the end of the horizon (taper the negative bias)."
  ]
}
